<h2 id="purpose-and-scope" style="color:#1565c0;font-size:1.22em;font-weight:650;border-left:4px solid #42a5f5;padding-left:10px;margin-top:1.1em">1. Purpose and scope</h2>

- **Portable:** This file is the **only** Cursor rule needed for war-story discipline when you copy **`exwar-war-stories-extraction.mdc`** together with **`.cursor/rules/war_stories_example/`** (**both** markdown exemplars) into another project; collection paths remain **`war_stories/`**–relative.
- **Convention:** Story text and project anchors live under **`war_stories/`** at the repository root (see [War stories directory](#war-stories-directory-portable-convention)). This rule names **only** those relative paths for **live** collections—never product-specific app directories embedded in portable prose.

<h3 id="purpose-audience-example" style="color:#00695c;font-size:1.05em;font-weight:600;margin-top:0.85em">1.1 Audience, tone, reference examples</h3>

**Who should apply this:**

- Humans or assistants **adding/editing entries** in **`war_stories/WAR_STORIES.md`** (and companion **`war_stories/WAR_STORIES_PROJECT_SPECIFIC.md`**).
- Turning **chat, failures, or debugging** into stories **before merge**—use this rule plus the **`exwar`** flow when extracting from Cursor history.

**Readability:**

- Readers skim **under time pressure**. **Short paragraphs**, **strong headings**, **payoff early** when possible—not long essays.
- **Length (soft recommendation only):** Aim for roughly **2–4 screenfuls of prose** per story when that still tells the lesson. **Being informative outranks brevity**—especially when [§10](#code-blocks) fenced snippets carry the mechanism; **`§N.4 Resolution`** may grow with code. There is **no hard word or character cap**.

**Example corpus (tone + structure, not verbatim copy destinations):**

Before drafting or reviewing **`exwar`** output, **read both** files under **`.cursor/rules/war_stories_example/`** so formatting, keyword flavor, and **§N.1–N.5** rhythm match project expectations:

| File | Role |
| --- | --- |
| **`WAR_STORIES_PORT_LEARN_SAMPLES.md`** | **Primary exemplar** for this repo: **short**, **Portuguese-learn–stack** stories mixing **technical** incidents (bugs, React state, keying) and **design** arcs (pipelines, contracts, autonomy). Use it first for **outline + story index + table/Mermaid** patterns aligned with **`mrkd`**. |
| **`WAR_STORIES_AWS.md`** | **Secondary / density reference**: longer **AWS-flavored** entries—**comparison tables**, **fenced blocks**, **stack-heavy** narration when you want extra calibration on richness or voice. |

**Do not** paste either exemplar verbatim into **`war_stories/WAR_STORIES.md`** unless you are intentionally archiving a corpus—port **patterns** (especially from **`WAR_STORIES_PORT_LEARN_SAMPLES.md`**).

**Story orientation:** Worthy captures include **technical-oriented** entries ([§5.1 Technical lessons](#technical-lessons)—how the stack behaved and what fixed it) **and** **design-oriented** entries ([§5.2 Design decisions](#design-decisions)—tradeoffs among alternatives, UX or API contracts, system boundaries). **Both kinds are explicitly in scope;** prioritize **signal and reusability**, not “bugs only” or “architecture only.” The exemplars **mix both** (`**keywords:**` ranges from **`react … bug, frontend`** to **`design, system design, …`**).

<h3 id="voice-and-register" style="color:#00695c;font-size:1.05em;font-weight:600;margin-top:0.85em">1.2 Voice and register</h3>

- **Write like a staff/principal architect narrating an internal postmortem or design review**: reason explicitly in **constraints**, **tradeoffs**, **invariants**, and **quantified impact**—tie the prose back to the story's own **`difficulty`** / **`significance`** numbers rather than asserting importance in the abstract. Keep language **plain** per [§1.1 Readability](#purpose-audience-example) and [§9](#readability-and-markdown-authoring); sophistication should show up as **precision**, not vocabulary.
- **No interview tells:** Never name-drop **`interview`**, "demonstrates my skill," "classic **X** question," "sound bite," or add an **`Interview prompts this answers`**-style Q&A block inside story prose (**Context** through **Takeaway**). The **senior-interview bar** in [§1.1](#purpose-audience-example) / [§3](#what-qualifies-meaningful-stories) decides **which** lessons are worth writing down—it must stay **invisible** in **how** they are phrased. The **`N.1`–`N.5`** structure (situation → mechanism → principle → decision → closing line) already carries that quality without saying so; let it.
- **First-person is out of register:** stories are written in the same **third-person / imperative** voice as the rest of the collection ("When **X** happens, do **Y**"), not "I did **X**" or "the story I tell." First person only belongs inside a genuine **quoted error message or log line**.
- **Self-review:** before finalizing, search the new story text for the literal string **`interview`**—if it appears anywhere in **`N.1`**–**`N.5`** prose (metadata **`keywords:`** included), rewrite that sentence to state the principle directly instead.

