<h2 id="workflow-live-conversation-online-extraction" style="color:#1565c0;font-size:1.22em;font-weight:650;border-left:4px solid #42a5f5;padding-left:10px;margin-top:1.1em">6. Workflow: live conversation (online extraction)</h2>

1. When the chat surfaces something that passes [What qualifies](#what-qualifies-meaningful-stories), **say briefly** why it qualifies (Tier A/B), **which file** ([§2.1](#war-stories-directory-portable-convention)), and ask whether to add **the next numbered story** (after ensuring [War stories directory](#war-stories-directory-portable-convention) exists).
2. If the user agrees, **append** one story in one edit: pick **N** after the latest story heading **`## N.`** or **`war-story-N`** in the collection, fill all subsections in [Entry structure and metadata](#entry-structure-and-metadata), and **refresh** any [Collection scaffolding](#collection-file-scaffolding) (outline / story index) that file already maintains.
3. If the user declines, **do not** write the file; a one-sentence takeaway in chat is optional.

---

<h2 id="workflow-batch-from-chat-history" style="color:#1565c0;font-size:1.22em;font-weight:650;border-left:4px solid #42a5f5;padding-left:10px;margin-top:1.1em">7. Workflow: batch from chat history</h2>

1. **Ingest** the provided history (or summary): chronological is fine; completeness matters more than length.
2. Emit **8–15 one-line candidates**, **Tier A first**, then Tier B, each tied to a concrete artifact when possible (path, command, metric, doc title).
3. Let the user **select or cap** how many to extract (e.g. top 5, or “only A”).
4. For each approved item, add **one** numbered story to the **routed** file ([§2.1](#war-stories-directory-portable-convention))—and **extend** that file’s [Collection scaffolding](#collection-file-scaffolding) in the same edit. Avoid merging unrelated threads into a single grab-bag story unless they share one **title-worthy** lesson.

<h3 id="workflow-hand-authoring" style="color:#00695c;font-size:1.05em;font-weight:600;margin-top:0.85em">7.1 Hand-authoring cadence (human or agent)</h3>

When drafting **without** live **`exwar`**, follow this sequence:

1. **Qualify** — passes [What qualifies](#what-qualifies-meaningful-stories)? If it is a trivial typo with no reusable lesson, skip.
2. **Collect artifacts** (while memory is fresh): exact commands / exit codes / short error text; paths, config keys, env vars, API paths, migration names; symptom vs **how you proved** the fix (test, command, manual check).
3. **Pick N** — next index after the latest story title in the target collection (**`## N.`** convention or **`id="war-story-N"`** anchor).
4. **Metadata first** — `creation`, `last_updated`, `keywords`, `difficulty`, `significance` (see [Entry structure](#entry-structure-and-metadata)).
5. **Fill subsections in order** — Context → Root Cause → Key Insight → Resolution → Takeaway, using evidence from step 2.
6. **Code blocks** — when the lesson is easier to reuse as code; trim to crucial lines with English comments ([§10](#code-blocks)).
7. **Visual aids** — tables / Mermaid / sparing inline HTML spans when they beat prose (**`mrkd`** §6 mandatory for tables/diagrams; see [Visual aids](#visual-aids)); **tier A / design** stories should bias toward comparison tables and phased-flow diagrams when the lesson is inherently multi-stage.
8. **Collection scaffolding** — if **`WAR_STORIES.md`** has a Document outline / story index, **extend it** in this same edit (see [§8.1](#collection-file-scaffolding)).
9. **Self-review** — [Writing quality](#writing-quality-rules) and [Checklist before merge](#checklist-before-merge).

