<h2 id="readability-and-markdown-authoring" style="color:#1565c0;font-size:1.22em;font-weight:650;border-left:4px solid #42a5f5;padding-left:10px;margin-top:1.1em">9. Readability and Markdown authoring</h2>

When **creating or editing** the war-story **collection** file (or companion project docs linked from stories), follow the repo’s **`.cursor/rules/mrkd-markdown-authoring.mdc`** as a **hard requirement**:

- Start long **`WAR_STORIES.md`** files with **`## Document outline`** (tight numbering; indented continuation lines **`5.1 [Subsection title](#anchor) — summary.`** mirroring **`mrkd`** §3—**do not** start a **nested Markdown ordered list** inside an outline row, which reintroduces spurious **`1.`**, **`2.`** markers).
- Use at most **`#` → `##` → `###`** (Markdown) **unless** the collection uses **`mrkd`**-palette **HTML headings** with explicit **`id`**, as allowed in [Entry structure §8](#entry-structure-and-metadata).
- Prefer **fenced code** with a **language tag**; use **`text`** for log excerpts.

**Collection-specific:** If **`WAR_STORIES.md`** already ships a reading guide plus story-index tables, treat them as **first-class artifacts**—update them whenever you append a story so navigation stays honest ([§8.1](#collection-file-scaffolding)).

If that rule file is unavailable or unreadable, **pause and ask the user how to proceed before finalizing the edit**. Do not silently downgrade formatting standards.

**Skim-first prose (collection files may use light HTML where the repo already does):**

- Slightly **larger or colored** emphasis for the main lesson or a warning is fine when it speeds scanning—**one or two accent colors** per story, not a rainbow.
- Prefer **full words** over unexplained abbreviations; define a stack-specific term **once** in Context if needed.
- Expert-expected terms (*migration*, *session cookie*, *CORS*) are fine when accurate; drop insider-only shorthand when a short plain phrase works.
- **Length vs code:** The [§1.1](#purpose-audience-example) soft length target yields to **clarity**—do not strip fences or shrink **§N.4** when commented snippets are the best teaching device.

---

<h2 id="code-blocks" style="color:#1565c0;font-size:1.22em;font-weight:650;border-left:4px solid #42a5f5;padding-left:10px;margin-top:1.1em">10. Code blocks and reusable snippets</h2>

**Priority:** When a lesson hinges on **how** something was implemented, prefer **curated fenced snippets** over long prose alone. **Informativeness** (including code) outranks informal length guidance ([§1.1](#purpose-audience-example), [§9](#readability-and-markdown-authoring)).

- **When to include:** Technical (and mixed) stories where the fix, contract, guard, API shape, or test pattern is easier to **reuse in another project** as code than as narrative alone. If plain sentences fully suffice, **skip** the fence.
- **Shape:** Leave layout to the author (before/after pair, single reference block, verification command, etc.). Show only **crucial** lines—trim boilerplate, imports, and noise—but keep enough context that a reader can adapt the pattern elsewhere.
- **Faithful project code:** Snippets reflect **actual** project code (real symbols and paths). Do **not** anonymize or rename modules solely because the story lives in **`WAR_STORIES.md`** ([§2.1](#war-stories-directory-portable-convention)). Repos are expected to become **public GitHub** projects; faithful excerpts are a feature, not a portability violation.
- **Inline comments:** Add **English-only** comments inside snippets for non-obvious lines (invariants, merge scope, “why not X,” failure modes). Skip comments that only restate the code.
- **Syntax highlighting:** Use a correct **language tag** on every opening fence (`bash`, `python`, `typescript`, `tsx`, `yaml`, `json`, `sql`, `text`, …) per **`mrkd`** §8—so previews colorize properly.
- **Where to put them:**
  - **Root Cause:** failing command, error fragment, bad config, bad response body.
  - **Resolution:** fixed command, corrected config or code, “after” snippet.
  - **Verification:** command (and optional short output) proving the fix.
- **Longer playbooks:** Put runnable scripts under **`war_stories/assets/`** ([§2](#war-stories-directory-portable-convention)); link from **§N.4** and keep the in-story fence to the **essential** pattern.

<h3 id="code-source-attribution" style="color:#00695c;font-size:1.05em;font-weight:600;margin-top:0.85em">10.1 Source attribution and GitHub links</h3>

Immediately **above** each project **source** fence—or once at the start of **§N.4** when all snippets share one file—cite:

1. **Project name** — short label (repository folder name, README title, or package name).
2. **Repo-relative path** — always present, e.g. `apps/api/app/routes/practice_workbench.py`.

**Optional Markdown hyperlink** to the file on GitHub—**only when the link is stable**:

<table>
<thead>
<tr style="background:#1565c0;color:white"><th>Use a GitHub link when</th><th>Path text only (no URL)</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td style="background:#e8f5e9"><code>git remote get-url origin</code> resolves to <code>https://github.com/{owner}/{repo}</code> (optional <code>.git</code> suffix)</td><td style="background:#ffebee">No <code>origin</code>, non-GitHub host, or unknown remote</td></tr>
<tr><td style="background:#e8f5e9">Default branch is known (<code>main</code>, <code>master</code>, or <code>origin/HEAD</code>)</td><td style="background:#ffebee">Default branch cannot be determined</td></tr>
<tr><td style="background:#e8f5e9">Path is **tracked** and durable (not <code>cursor_gen/</code> or scratch-only)</td><td style="background:#ffebee">Ephemeral or unpublished-only paths ([§12](#writing-quality-rules))</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

**Link shape (when stable):** `[filename](https://github.com/{owner}/{repo}/blob/{branch}/{repo-relative-path}#L{start}-L{end})` — add `#L`–`L` when the fence maps to contiguous source lines. Line anchors **may drift** after later edits; path + inlined snippet remain authoritative.

**When not stable:** Do **not** guess or ship broken URLs. Use plain text, e.g. **`my-project`** · `path/to/file.py`.

**Example pattern:** One attribution line, then the fenced snippet (language-tagged). The attribution might read: **`portuguese-learn`** · linked `practice_workbench.py` pointing at `https://github.com/{owner}/{repo}/blob/main/apps/api/.../practice_workbench.py#L40-L55`, followed by a language-tagged fence containing the trimmed excerpt and **English** inline comments on non-obvious lines.

