<h2 id="writing-quality-rules" style="color:#1565c0;font-size:1.22em;font-weight:650;border-left:4px solid #42a5f5;padding-left:10px;margin-top:1.1em">12. Writing quality rules</h2>

- Name **real** files, commands, endpoints, settings, or metrics—not only “the pipeline.”
- **Project code citations:** Pair snippets with **project name + repo-relative path**; add a **GitHub blob link** only when [§10.1](#code-source-attribution) stability checks pass—otherwise path text only.
- State **how you verified** the fix or decision (command, test, benchmark, doc link).
- Prefer **reusable guardrails** (“next time, validate X before Y”) over narrative alone.
- Avoid blame; describe **behavior** and **constraints**.
- Write in the **architect voice** from [§1.2](#voice-and-register); keep the senior-interview qualification bar out of the prose itself.
- **Portable body text:** Critical decisions summarized from **scratch plans**, **research docs**, **`cursor_gen/`** drafts, or **local-only notebooks** belong **inside** **`§N.4`** (tables, steps, bullets)—after those sources are trimmed or omitted from git. Readers must not hit **404 anchors** inside the repo tomorrow.
- **No explicit `cursor_gen/` references in shipped stories:** absorb the content, but do not cite `cursor_gen/*` paths, filenames, or anchors in final **`WAR_STORIES.md`** / **`WAR_STORIES_PROJECT_SPECIFIC.md`** entries. Treat `cursor_gen/` as an authoring workspace, not a durable citation target.

